// pkg/orm — the Data interface (#5055): what Pool and a live transaction
// share, so an ORM function runs standalone or inside a caller's
// transaction with no second version and no second entry point.
//
//     fn transfer(db: Data, from: i64, to: i64, amt: decimal): ()! { ... }
//
//     transfer(pool, 1, 2, amt)?                       // its own transaction, per statement
//     pool.tx((db) => { transfer(db, 1, 2, amt)? })?    // inside the caller's
//
// `Data` is `query`/`exec` — the same shape std/sql's own `Executor`
// (stdlib/sql/tx.bit) already names, under the ORM's vocabulary: every ORM
// function that reads or writes takes `Data`, never `Pool`, so nothing in
// this package has to be written twice for "runs on its own" and "runs in a
// transaction".
//
// `db.tx(...)`/`db.txAt(...)` (owner decision 2026-09-12: "aesthetically
// unpleasing" about `tx(pool, f)`) are METHODS on `Pool` now, not free
// functions a caller has to import separately — stdlib/sql/txmethods.bit's
// `Transactable` trait, `use`d once by `Pool` (stdlib/sql/pool.bit). This
// file does not repeat that shape: std/sql's own `tx`/`txAt`/`txValue`/
// `txValueAt` (stdlib/sql/tx.bit) are unchanged and still exported for a
// caller that has not moved to the method form.
//
// THE CLOSURE PARAMETER SHADOWS THE OUTER HANDLE ON PURPOSE. Naming it `db`
// again, as every example above does, makes the pool underneath
// unreachable inside the block — `pool.tx((db) => { pool.save(a)? })` would
// write outside the transaction and never roll back if `pool` stayed
// reachable under a different name. The shortest correct spelling is also
// the safe one.
import { Executor } from "std/sql"

// Anything ORM code can run a query or a write against: a `Pool`, or the
// handle of a transaction already running on one. See this file's header
// for why every ORM function takes `Data` instead of `Pool`.
export type Data = Executor

// #5384's DECISION: `Data` DOES NOT CARRY A DIALECT/VERSION DESCRIPTOR.
// Three sites (`write.bit`'s `upsert`, `lock.bit`'s `forUpdate`,
// `manytomany.bit`'s `attach`/`sync`) had each grown their own hand-passed
// tag for "which server", and a fourth (`runner.bit`'s advisory lock) had
// none at all and stayed silently Postgres-only. Widening `Data` was
// rejected: `Data` is a bare alias to std/sql's own `Executor`
// (this file's header) — a real descriptor field would mean either
// growing `Executor` itself (stdlib, not this package's to change) or
// wrapping every `Pool`/`Tx` construction site in a new type this package
// does not own the construction of, OR asking the live connection what it
// is, which the ticket forbids outright (no query behind the caller's
// back). Both routes reach outside `pkg/orm`'s own files for a fact only
// the caller already knows when it opens the connection.
//
// SO EVERY SITE TAKES IT EXPLICITLY, AND THEY NOW SHARE ONE SHAPE:
// `ServerDialect` below, not four. `LockDialect` (`lock.bit`, #5391) was
// already strictly more informative than `UpsertDialect` (`write.bit`,
// #5058) — a bare tag is a version-less special case of tag-plus-version —
// so the richer shape absorbs the poorer one instead of `Data` absorbing
// either.
//
// BEHAVIOR CHANGE, NOT JUST A RENAME: `LockDialect.Neutral` does not
// survive the merge. It covered Postgres AND MySQL 8+ with no version
// check at all — a caller could pass it against a MySQL server and get
// `SKIP LOCKED`/`NOWAIT` through unchecked, asserting "trust me, this is
// 8.0+" with nothing behind the assertion. `ServerDialect.Postgres` covers
// only Postgres; every MySQL call now states its real `MysqlVersion`,
// checked on every `forUpdate`. A caller who was relying on `Neutral`'s
// bypass will not compile until it supplies a version. That is a strictly
// safer default, not a narrower one, and it is deliberate: it is the
// trade #5384 asked for, not a side effect of the rename.
//
// THE COST, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: `write.bit`'s `upsert` and
// `manytomany.bit`'s `attach`/`sync` never needed a version — `ON
// CONFLICT` vs `ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE` is a syntax choice, not a
// version-gated one — so a caller targeting MySQL for either now supplies
// a `MysqlVersion` those two call paths never read. Cheaper than a fifth
// shape, and no worse than a caller having to know its own server's
// version at all, which every MySQL-targeting call already required at
// `lock.bit`'s site.
//
// `runner.bit`'s advisory lock takes `ServerDialect` explicitly too, as an
// `up`/`down` parameter — never derived by inspecting the unrelated
// `Dialect` (`dialect.bit`) those functions already take for DDL
// rendering. The two answer different questions (which server locks it;
// which renderer builds its DDL) and reading one off the other's concrete
// type would be exactly the hidden coupling that let a real MySQL run
// silently send `pg_advisory_lock` to a server that has no such function
// — `RunnerDialect` (`runner.bit`) bundles the two fields into one
// parameter only to satisfy `bit lint`'s max-params finding, never
// collapses them into one. See `runner.bit`'s own header, piece 2, for
// what "explicit" means there: no MySQL advisory-lock implementation
// exists yet (unstarted, separate work), so `up`/`down` refuse outright
// rather than issuing a statement no MySQL server accepts.
//
// A MySQL server's reported version, as a caller targeting MySQL already
// knows it — nothing in this package looks it up itself (paragraph above).
export class MysqlVersion {
  export major: int,
  export minor: int,
}

// Which server every dialect-sensitive ORM call is targeting — the one
// shape `write.bit`, `lock.bit`, `manytomany.bit` and `runner.bit` all take
// explicitly now (paragraph above). `Mysql` always carries its version:
// `lock.bit`'s guard needs it to gate `SKIP LOCKED`/`NOWAIT` on MySQL 8.0;
// a call site that only needs the Postgres/MySQL syntax choice (`upsert`,
// `attach`, `sync`) matches the tag and ignores the payload.
export enum ServerDialect {
  Postgres,
  Mysql(MysqlVersion),
}
